#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055844} :activity/id #uuid "0e31a49b-f54b-50cd-ab36-02966e00c737" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/id #uuid "ac718bb5-4681-5ba0-883a-072801cad492" :topic/math-academy-id 35} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055962 :tutorial/content "For a function $f(x)$, we can express the derivative as $f'(x)$.\n\nRecall that we can also express the derivative using the **differential operator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nWe can use the differential operator while computing the derivative, too. For example, the power rule can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left(x^n \\right) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\big(\\cdots\\big)\n$$\n\njust means that we need to differentiate whatever is inside $\\big(\\cdots\\big)$ with respect to $x$." :tutorial/id #uuid "537fb074-19a0-542c-9a84-c4542e26b33f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2647 :tutorial/title "The Differential Operator"} :step/id #uuid "219dccb2-c3f8-5ef8-af33-9ea8f7e5c0f8" :step/math-academy-id 12596 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055978 :tutorial/content "We know how to differentiate a function like\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nusing the definition of the derivative. However, there is a trick to do it faster!\n\nThe trick is called the **power rule**, and it states that for a general power function in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nthe derivative is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nThe function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\ntakes the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^n\n$$\n\nwith $n=2$, so we can use the power rule to compute its derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= nx^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2 - 1} \\\\\n&= 2x^{1} \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result agrees with what we would get if we used the definition of the derivative. However, using the power rule, the computations are much easier!" :tutorial/id #uuid "37492dee-669b-5eb2-9676-b78afaf6acb4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 337 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ac48d370-ba29-5343-bf37-4b82f3611773" :step/math-academy-id 625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054455 :question/id #uuid "67ce214a-e9e9-5a32-b48c-d9488cb141ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32dd6880-3725-564a-9e85-0d39b6960c72" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055196}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Power Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "ea42d926-8045-5d22-9aa7-2fd3a3f233c8" :step/math-academy-id 626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054456 :question/id #uuid "54c18c81-9deb-5958-81a0-1abecf91ba58" :question/math-academy-id "e-135"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2ff1808-d239-5625-93b7-21a98ada3fab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050495}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055347}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Negative Power Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "8893f4d9-c611-591e-80a2-799835854aae" :step/math-academy-id 629 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054457 :question/id #uuid "1717a513-6866-5b4e-8c9a-1ea7f343acab" :question/math-academy-id "e-136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d06cd8-9aae-50ab-90dc-6c70b545d569" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050894}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234083}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Fractional Power Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "41c0eff0-f259-5568-b570-bafddf204af8" :step/math-academy-id 631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055983 :tutorial/content "In general, if\n\n$$\nf(x) = k\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant, then $f'(x) = 0$. There are a couple ways to see why this is.\n\nFirst, let's take the constant function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2\n$$\n\nbut write it a little differently:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2x^0\n$$\n\nTo differentiate the function above, we can use the power rule with $n=0$. Applying the power rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 0 \\cdot 2x^{0 - 1} \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 2x^{-1} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere's nothing special about the number $2$. The same result would hold for *any* constant function.\n\nWe can also think about this geometrically. The function $y=f(x)=2$ is a horizontal line, and it does not rise or fall as we change $x$. So, its slope is zero everywhere, and therefore $f'(x) = 0$ everywhere.\n\n![](images/22/222febb3babc289ba15ba1e187aa9848ea6f2d802bf21ad8e1396b092370d564.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "aba9943d-6c1b-5f0d-b9e0-a0c854c231b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 341 :tutorial/title "The Derivative of the Constant Function"} :step/id #uuid "421f20ea-c000-57af-973a-3496371dd497" :step/math-academy-id 633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055849}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054465 :question/id #uuid "27baaab1-2088-5277-be24-5dfba2bfa5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-137"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "654f8868-880b-53dc-ba4c-677aecb10b95" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048262}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055343}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Constant Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "814ed39b-61cd-5eb5-92ed-72ff4dbe9a1b" :step/math-academy-id 634}] :activity/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054897}]}
